.TH INSTALL-LOGIN.SH 1 "2026-09-10" "mu2edaq-shifter-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
install-login.sh \- install the shifter login environment and commands
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B install-login.sh
[\fB\-d\fR \fIhome\fR]
[\fB\-b\fR \fIbin\fR]
[\fB\-r\fR \fIroot\fR]
[\fB\-N\fR]
[\fB\-n\fR]
[\fB\-h\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B install-login.sh
deploys the toolkit into a shifter account:
.TP
.I login/*
installed into the home directory with a leading dot, so
\fIlogin/bashrc\fR becomes \fI~/.bashrc\fR, mode 644.
.TP
.I scripts/*
installed into \fI~/bin\fR, mode 755. This is why a helper that
shifters should be able to invoke by name belongs in \fIscripts/\fR.
.TP
.I start-daq.sh, stop-daq.sh
the top-level start/stop pair, also installed into \fI~/bin\fR.
.TP
.I ~/.mu2edaq/env
written with \fBMU2EDAQ_ROOT\fR set to this checkout.
.PP
That last file matters: a command sitting in \fI~/bin\fR cannot find
\fIconfig/\fR and \fIdata/\fR by walking up from itself, so the root is
recorded where both
.BR daq-common.sh (3)
and the Python config module look for it. Values in that file rank
below the real environment, so exporting \fBMU2EDAQ_ROOT\fR still wins.
.PP
\fI~/bin\fR is added to \fBPATH\fR by the installed
\fI~/.bash_profile\fR, so the commands appear after the next login.
.PP
Every file that would be overwritten is first copied to
\fI<file>.bak.<timestamp>\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
Configuration precedence is command line, then environment variable,
then built-in default.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-home " " \fIDIR\fR
Install the dotfiles into \fIDIR\fR. Environment:
\fBMU2EDAQ_INSTALL_HOME\fR. Default: \fB$HOME\fR.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bin " " \fIDIR\fR
Install the commands into \fIDIR\fR. Environment:
\fBMU2EDAQ_INSTALL_BIN\fR. Default: \fI<home>/bin\fR.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-root " " \fIDIR\fR
Record \fIDIR\fR as \fBMU2EDAQ_ROOT\fR instead of this checkout. Useful
when installing from a build tree that will be moved.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-no\-env
Do not write \fI<home>/.mu2edaq/env\fR.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print every action and change nothing.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.SH NOTES
The installed \fI~/.bash_profile\fR performs the Kerberos and git
bootstrap in a fixed order that must not be rearranged; see
.BR get_krb_daq_principal.sh (1)
and the ARCHITECTURE document for why.
.PP
Renaming \fBget_krb_daq_principle.sh\fR to
\fBget_krb_daq_principal.sh\fR means an account installed from an older
checkout keeps a stale copy of the old name in \fI~/bin\fR. It is inert
and can be deleted.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success or after a dry run, 1 if \fIlogin/\fR or
\fIscripts/\fR is missing, and 2 for an unrecognised option.
.SH EXAMPLES
See exactly what would be installed:
.PP
.RS
.nf
./install-login.sh --dry-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Install for real:
.PP
.RS
.nf
./install-login.sh
.fi
.RE
.PP
Stage an install into a scratch directory:
.PP
.RS
.nf
./install-login.sh -d /tmp/stage -b /tmp/stage/bin
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bootstrap.sh (1),
.BR get_krb_principal.sh (1),
.BR get_krb_daq_principal.sh (1),
.BR set_git_env.sh (1),
.BR daq-common.sh (3)
